# __REPO_NAME__ — operator orientation

**Sync-managed** (PROJ-039/T-095). Do not edit this file in place — it is rendered from
`agent-tooling/scaffold/home-repo-README.template` by scaffold.sh / sync-agent-tooling.sh
and any local edit is overwritten on the next TOOLING_UPDATE. Propose changes as an
agent-tooling PR against the template.

This file is for **you, the operator** — where to look, not how the agent works.
Agent-facing instructions live in `AGENTS.md` (and, for the team-lead variant,
`OPERATING-MANUAL.md`); this README never duplicates them.

## What this repo is

This is __AGENT__'s **home repo** — the __ROLE_TITLE__ agent on the __TEAM__ team. It
carries __AGENT__'s session lifecycle: hooks fire automatically at session start/stop/end,
and most of what lands here is written by those hooks, not typed by hand. It is **not**
the team repo (`__TEAM_REPO__`) — that holds the team's shared planning surface and is
a separate clone.

## Who writes here

- **Session hooks** (`.claude/hooks/`) — write session results, telemetry, and the
  `.workspace/` scratch context automatically at each session boundary. This is most of
  the activity you'll see in the commit log.
- **__AGENT__**, during a session — commits code/doc changes per the brief it was
  dispatched to work.
- **The team lead**, when dispatching — drops a brief file under `team-lead/briefs/`
  (see below).
- **`agent-tooling` sync** — updates `.claude/` (hooks, lib, skills, tools) and this
  README/manual on a `TOOLING_UPDATE`. Never hand-edit `.claude/` — it is overwritten on
  the next sync.

## Directory structure

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `.claude/` | Resident tooling — hooks, `lib/`, `primitives/`, `tools/`, (team-lead only) `skills/`. Sync-managed from `agent-tooling`; do not hand-edit. |
| `results/` | One file per session — the durable session record (`session-{date}-{work-item}-{sid8}.md`). This is the first place to look for "what did this agent do." |
| `session-reports/` | Supplementary per-session reporting output, where the role produces it. |
| `memory/` | The agent's persistent memory files (if the runtime uses file-based memory). |
| `team-lead/briefs/` | Dispatch records — the brief this agent was/is working, one file per brief. The human/audit copy; the live dispatch record is the cloud `briefs` collection point. |
| `logs/` | **Local working state only** — gitignored, not committed. The durable copy of a completed session's logs lives in the `agent-telemetry` repo, not here. |
| `.workspace/` | **Ephemeral** — materialised brief/task context plus any on-demand repo clones for the current session. Gitignored; wiped and re-materialised each session. Never durable state. |

## How agent activity is recorded — the session trail, end to end

1. **Dispatch** — the team lead authors a brief and creates/approves a point in the
   cloud `briefs` Qdrant collection. The human copy lands in this repo's
   `team-lead/briefs/{date}-{slug}.md`.
2. **Launch** — the session runs, keyed to a runtime session id (`sid`); the brief point
   records that sid in `session_ids[]`.
3. **Commits** — where the work actually lands, per this repo's lifecycle mode (below):
   trunk mode commits land directly on `main`; branch mode commits land on a session
   branch that is merged via a result PR.
4. **Result** — at session end, the finalise hook authors `results/session-{date}-
   {work-item}-{sid8}.md` — the durable, human-readable record of what happened, and
   lands it per lifecycle mode (direct commit on trunk, or as part of the result PR on
   branch mode).
5. **Registry** — the team lead's consolidation pass rows this session into the team
   repo's `planning/sessions/active.md`, then rolls status into
   `planning/team-tasklist.md` + `planning/STATUS.md`.
6. **Telemetry** — session/tool-use events land in cloud Qdrant (`sessions` rollups,
   `claude_session_telemetry`); full transcripts/logs are pushed to the
   `agent-telemetry` repo. This is the place to look for low-level detail beyond the
   result file.

So: brief → commits → result file → registry row → STATUS → telemetry, in that order,
is the full trail for any piece of agent work.

## The change-visibility policy — which changes arrive as a PR, and which don't

Decided 2026-07-10; applies fleet-wide:

- **Home repos** (this repo) — agent sessions are hook-managed: commits/results push to
  the default branch **directly, no PR**. A direct-to-main commit here is normal
  operation, not an incident.
- **Team repos** — a team lead's own adjustments to its own team repo push to the
  default branch **directly, no PR** too (this generalises what used to be an
  apex-only privilege to every lead, on their own team repo).
- **Working repos** — any repo checked out under a session's `.workspace/` and modified
  per a brief's instructions (including when that repo happens to be a home or team
  repo, because the brief specifically targeted it) goes through the **PR path**. These
  are not operational hook-driven changes — they're briefed work product.
- **Rare exception:** a brief or operator instruction can explicitly target the CURRENT
  operational team/home repo you're standing on ("re-engineering the beam we are
  standing on"). **Caveat:** any change made this way to an agent-tooling **manifest
  item** (a file `sync-agent-tooling.sh` manages) is overwritten on the next
  `TOOLING_UPDATE` — such a change is not durable unless it also lands as an
  agent-tooling PR.

In short: if you see a commit straight on `main` here, that's the policy working as
designed, not a bypassed review.

## Lifecycle mode — trunk vs branch

This repo runs one of two modes, recorded at `.claude/tooling-manifest.json` →
`lifecycle_mode` (absent means `branch`, the default):

- **`branch`** — a session launches on a fresh session branch, commits land there, and
  session end raises a **result PR** back to `main`.
- **`trunk`** — a session locks the repo (no branch), commits land **directly on
  `main`** as the session progresses, and session end commits the result directly too
  (no PR). This is the model behind the direct-to-main commits described above.

Check the manifest field directly for this repo's current mode — it can change as
repos are migrated.
